Currently, we have the following function for constructing a TensorImpl* from a Tensor:
inline at::Tensor THTensor_wrap(THTensor* tensor) {
  c10::raw::intrusive_ptr::incref(tensor);
  return at::Tensor(c10::intrusive_ptr<at::TensorImpl>::reclaim(tensor));
}

(NB: THTensor is a TensorImpl)
This creates a new owning copy of Tensor, necessitating a refcount bump on Tensor.
This is quite distressing, because in many cases the THTensor* in question would have stayed live for the entire time at::Tensor was live, and we shouldn't have introduced a refcount bump at all in this case. We'd like a way to create a const at::Tensor&, without causing a refcount bump. Is it possible to do this? A major barrier to this is the way Tensor is implemented today, which is effectively equivalent to the class below:
class Tensor {
  intrusive_ptr<TensorImpl> impl_;
}

The destructor of intrusive_ptr<TensorImpl> decrements the refcount; thus, there is no way to create a Tensor without causing an impending refcount decref.
Here is one possible strategy which could resolve the problem, taking advantage of the fact that Tensor is used purely as a value class. Restructure Tensor in the following way:

class Tensor {
  TensorImpl* impl_;
  // NB: Purposely non-virtual
  ~Tensor() {
    c10::raw::intrusive_ptr::decref(impl_);
  }
}

class UnsafeNonOwningTensor : public Tensor {
  UnsafeNonOwningTensor(TensorImpl* impl) : impl_(impl) {}
  ~UnsafeNonOwningTensor() {
    impl_ = UndefinedTensorImpl::singleton();
  }
}


The idea is to subclass Tensor into a class that disables the decref of the parent class by reassigning the raw pointer to the undefined tensor (aka nullptr). Because UnsafeNonOwningTensor inherits from Tensor, an UnsafeNonOwningTensor is convertible into a Tensor. However, because UnsafeNonOwningTensor's destructor runs first, it avoids the decref by setting the pointer to something that is (morally) null.
We don't want to use UnsafeNonOwningTensor directly, since both references and rvalue references to UnsafeNonOwningTensor are convertible to references/rvalue references to Tensor. We only want to permit conversion to const Tensor reference. So we introduce another class which declares only the desired implicit conversion:
class TensorRef {
public:
  TensorRef(TensorImpl* p) : ref_(p);
  operator const Tensor&() const {
    return ref_;
  }
private:
  UnsafeNonOwningTensor ref_;
}

Now, to create a reference, you write:
TensorImpl* p; // input
do_some_operation(TensorRef(p));

p can now be treated as a const Tensor&.

🐛 Bug
On my Windows 10 machine, the traced model from the tutorial can not be executed. Calling auto fwd = module->forward({ tensor_image }); crashes the application and a debug assertion from the Microsoft Visual C++ Runtime Library is shown.

To Reproduce
Steps to reproduce the behavior:

Save a traced model (I used this for MNIST)
Try the execute a forward pass with the loaded model

cv::Mat image;
image = cv::imread(imagePath, CV_LOAD_IMAGE_GRAYSCALE);

std::ifstream in(modelPath, std::ios_base::binary);
if (!in.good()) {
	std::cerr << "Loading failed" << std::endl;
} else {
	std::cout << "Binary file read success" << std::endl;
}

// Deserialize the ScriptModule from a file using torch::jit::load().
std::shared_ptr<torch::jit::script::Module> module = torch::jit::load(in);
std::cout << "Module loaded" << std::endl;
std::vector<int64_t> sizes = { 1, 1, image.rows, image.cols };
at::TensorOptions options(at::ScalarType::Byte);
at::Tensor tensor_image = torch::from_blob(image.data, at::IntList(sizes), options);
tensor_image = tensor_image.toType(at::kFloat);
std::cout << "Executing forward()" << std::endl;
auto fwd = module->forward({ tensor_image });
std::cout << "Forward() done" << std::endl;
at::Tensor result = module->forward({ tensor_image }).toTensor();
std::cout << "Got result" << std::endl;
